WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.100
Hey Thoughty2 here.

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:08.840
Welcome to the Vidocq Society, a secret meeting
of the best criminal detectives in the world.

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:13.460
Where the world's most difficult crimes are
solved over a bowl of soup.

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:20.380
The society was formed in 1990 by three leading
ex-criminal psychologists and forensics experts,

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:22.009
all since retired.

00:00:22.009 --> 00:00:26.829
They would meet for lunch on the third Thursday
of every month in a private dining room on

00:00:26.829 --> 00:00:30.960
the top floor of the Union League Building
in Philadelphia.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:36.510
They started off merely discussing crime but
within a year they were solving them.

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:41.850
The now highly revered Vidocq Society quickly
grew to contain some of the world's best and

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:49.820
most experienced FBI profilers, homicide detectives,
scientists, psychologists, prosecutors, coroners

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:52.440
and forensic experts.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:58.470
All pay a $100 yearly fee and meet once a
month for lunch in the same secret dining

00:00:58.470 --> 00:00:59.570
room.

00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:05.100
Police chiefs, detectives and other law enforcement
officials travel from all over the world to

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:09.549
present their cold cases to the society over
lunch.

00:01:09.549 --> 00:01:15.350
Once all the facts and evidence has been presented
to the group, they will attempt to solve it.

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:21.499
The society has prerequisites, to present
a case it must be over 2 years old and unsolved

00:01:21.499 --> 00:01:24.240
and involve at least one murder.

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:30.960
The society doesn't charge for their services
and pays for all travel and hotel expenses

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:33.510
for the visiting law enforcement agent.

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:39.770
The society has gained fame worldwide amongst
law enforcement, for they have solved hundreds

00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:46.429
of the world's most complex cold cases, which
were previously thought unsolvable and simply

00:01:46.429 --> 00:01:48.850
filed away for years.

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:54.249
They have even freed innocent convicts who
were wrongly accused.

00:01:54.249 --> 00:01:57.659
But how is this possible?

00:01:57.659 --> 00:02:03.139
How come some detectives are better at solving
crimes than others and what secret techniques

00:02:03.139 --> 00:02:04.139
do they use?

00:02:04.139 --> 00:02:08.619
One of the best tools in the police's arsenal
is forensic science.

00:02:08.619 --> 00:02:12.010
It was first used in 1983 in England.

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:17.760
And has since become to go-to tool for solving
all kinds of crime worldwide.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:24.570
But two of the world's best forensics laboratories
can be found where you would least expect

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:25.909
them.

00:02:25.909 --> 00:02:31.970
Target, yes the very same Target that sells
jumpers and televisions.

00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:39.260
Retail giant Target has two of the world's
most advanced and most active forensics laboratories.

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:45.569
Located in secret back rooms in two Target
stores, in Las Vegas and Minneapolis, the

00:02:45.569 --> 00:02:51.159
Target forensics labs contain world experts
in the realm of forensic science and a myriad

00:02:51.159 --> 00:02:53.420
of expensive equipment.

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:58.370
Target's labs are fully accredited by the
American Society of Crime Laboratory Directors,

00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:03.840
of which there are only 390 such labs in America.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:10.069
These unusual crime solving powerhouses are
used to solve crimes such as theft, cyber-crime,

00:03:10.069 --> 00:03:13.110
fraud, murder and rape.

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:15.189
But why?

00:03:15.189 --> 00:03:21.560
Well, when you have a chain of over 1,800
discount super stores, each brimming with

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:28.750
expensive technology and other goods, you're
going to get a lot of crime and they do.

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:35.689
Target stores are host to hundreds of crimes
each week, ranging from theft, to sexual assault

00:03:35.689 --> 00:03:37.500
and even murder.

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:42.129
Local police forces are already overburdened
and especially their forensics labs.

00:03:42.129 --> 00:03:47.750
The average police force's forensics laboratory
is massively backlogged with evidence to process

00:03:47.750 --> 00:03:51.299
and examine and a pile of crimes to get through.

00:03:51.299 --> 00:03:56.680
If Target had to wait for the police to solve
every crime that effects their company or

00:03:56.680 --> 00:04:04.050
takes place in one of their stores they would
be waiting a long, long time for any progress.

00:04:04.050 --> 00:04:10.670
So it's much more effective to do it themselves,
being a private company they have the freedom

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:15.730
to spend far more money on their forensics
labs than the police do on theirs.

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:21.019
As a result Target has much more up-to-date
equipment than the actual police.

00:04:21.019 --> 00:04:24.390
And they can solve crimes rapidly.

00:04:24.390 --> 00:04:28.740
Once sufficient evidence has been found and
verified against a particular suspect, via

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:34.190
Target's forensics teams, it is handed over
to the police who will make an arrest and

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:36.310
convict the perpetrator.

00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:40.530
Target's evidence will then be used against
them in court.

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:46.460
Most of the forensics work conducted at Target's
labs is digital forensics, such as examining

00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:52.240
CCTV footage, retrieving lost data from smartphones
and computer hard drives and even examining

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:56.990
suspect's emails and internet traffic using
hacking.

00:04:56.990 --> 00:05:01.930
Target even possess a device called a Faraday
cage which they put a suspect's phone inside

00:05:01.930 --> 00:05:06.560
and it stops the data on the phone from being
remotely erased.

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:12.570
But they also do plenty of traditional forensics
too, such as fingerprint analysis and facial

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:15.870
recognition, even DNA matching.

00:05:15.870 --> 00:05:22.030
But Target are so good and fast at what they
do that police forces from all over the country

00:05:22.030 --> 00:05:27.760
commonly send their evidence to Target to
get their help in solving a crime.

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:31.110
And the best bit, Target do all this for free.

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:36.919
An employee said that one quarter of crimes
solved by Target's forensics labs are not

00:05:36.919 --> 00:05:41.790
related to Target in any way, yet they never
ask for compensation.

00:05:41.790 --> 00:05:48.250
For example, police in Houston asked Target
to help them solve a brutal arson case, in

00:05:48.250 --> 00:05:52.700
which a mother and her two children had been
torched to death.

00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:56.080
The police had hit a dead end with their investigations.

00:05:56.080 --> 00:06:01.509
They sent a CCTV tape from a nearby petrol
station to Target's forensics lab.

00:06:01.509 --> 00:06:07.400
The tape was wrinkled and unplayable, but
Target's top video analysts carefully placed

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:12.030
the tape between two pieces of rice paper
and ironed it.

00:06:12.030 --> 00:06:16.560
They then digitally enhanced the footage and
when they watched it they identified a high

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:22.270
school student buying a can of petrol just
moments before the arson occurred.

00:06:22.270 --> 00:06:29.569
Using this evidence the police were able to
convict the teenager of arson in court.

00:06:29.569 --> 00:06:35.691
So Target are damn good at targeting criminals
using traditional methods and some digital

00:06:35.691 --> 00:06:42.740
know-how but there are enforcement agencies
such as the FBI, MI5 and even some forward-thinking

00:06:42.740 --> 00:06:49.699
local law enforcement agencies, that are using
new, groundbreaking techniques and very clever

00:06:49.699 --> 00:06:53.789
technology to catch criminals like never before.

00:06:53.789 --> 00:06:59.319
The Federal Bureau of Investigations has always
been well prepared to solve any crime.

00:06:59.319 --> 00:07:08.589
The FBI has a collection of every single firearm
ever made, over 7,000 in total.

00:07:08.589 --> 00:07:14.460
So they can identify the exact make and model
any evidential weapons that appear during

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:17.830
cases.

00:07:17.830 --> 00:07:26.680
They have even created a database of precisely
what marking each gun leaves on a bullet when

00:07:26.680 --> 00:07:32.090
it is fired, which they have freely shared
with law enforcement agencies in most other

00:07:32.090 --> 00:07:33.090
countries.

00:07:33.090 --> 00:07:39.419
This means when someone gets shot they can
quickly find out exactly what gun they where

00:07:39.419 --> 00:07:45.729
shot with and even narrow down where the shooter
might have purchased the firearm from.

00:07:45.729 --> 00:07:50.949
Up until recently the techniques used to solve
crimes have remained the same for hundreds

00:07:50.949 --> 00:07:55.349
of years, effective interviewing of both witnesses
and suspects.

00:07:55.349 --> 00:07:59.729
Knocking on doors to follow up leads with
your cop buddy, True Detective style.

00:07:59.729 --> 00:08:05.319
And in more recent years, forensics of crime
scenes, weapons and bodies, including DNA

00:08:05.319 --> 00:08:06.319
matching.

00:08:06.319 --> 00:08:12.560
Though many futuristic techniques used in
your usual TV cop drama, such as blowing up

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:18.180
a low-quality image to many times its size
and somehow, miraculously enhancing it, so

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:25.759
that very fine detail can be now seen, just
doesn't exist, that isn't possible.

00:08:25.759 --> 00:08:32.159
TV shows certainly take a lot of liberties
in exaggerating the capabilities of the police,

00:08:32.159 --> 00:08:35.400
but maybe not for long.

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:42.890
Now police are looking towards computer scientists
to solve crimes and even predict when and

00:08:42.890 --> 00:08:48.570
where a crime is going to happen, before it
actually happens.

00:08:48.570 --> 00:08:55.240
In 2013 an MIT professor teamed up with two
analysts from Cambridge police to develop

00:08:55.240 --> 00:08:56.630
a computer algorithm.

00:08:56.630 --> 00:09:00.380
An algorithm that would solve crime.

00:09:00.380 --> 00:09:06.030
It's called Series Finder and it specialises
in attempting to solve home break-ins and

00:09:06.030 --> 00:09:07.260
burglaries.

00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:14.959
The algorithm uses data from 5,000 house break-ins
in Cambridge, between 1997 and 2006.

00:09:14.959 --> 00:09:19.490
It studies pieces of information such as the
time and date of the incident, how long it

00:09:19.490 --> 00:09:25.110
took, the means of entry to the property,
what they did once inside, what they took

00:09:25.110 --> 00:09:26.250
etc.

00:09:26.250 --> 00:09:31.560
Using all this data, the algorithm finds patterns
that would take human analysts many years

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:35.190
of sifting through masses of documents to
discover.

00:09:35.190 --> 00:09:40.400
The results can be used to narrow down who
might have done it, was it an organised criminal

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:41.400
gang?

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:42.790
Did they act alone?

00:09:42.790 --> 00:09:46.060
Did they come from out of town or are they
a local?

00:09:46.060 --> 00:09:51.990
House break-ins are notoriously hard to solve,
because of a lack of witnesses and there is

00:09:51.990 --> 00:09:55.769
usually no evidence left behind, just objects
missing.

00:09:55.769 --> 00:10:00.970
According to figures only 13% of residential
burglaries are solved.

00:10:00.970 --> 00:10:08.120
But this algorithm unearths data from these
crimes that was previously thought impossible.

00:10:08.120 --> 00:10:13.530
This incredibly clever computer software hasn't
yet provided enough evidence to convict someone

00:10:13.530 --> 00:10:21.050
of a home break-in, but amazingly it did completely
rule out one suspect from a real-life crime,

00:10:21.050 --> 00:10:24.690
whom police believed was the main suspect.

00:10:24.690 --> 00:10:29.240
The creators are now working on adapting the
system to solve other crimes such as rape

00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:35.190
and murder and they say it's only a matter
of time before their algorithm can actually

00:10:35.190 --> 00:10:40.500
solve the majority of crimes and convict criminals.

00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:47.550
But what about predicting crimes before they
happen, Minority Report style?

00:10:47.550 --> 00:10:52.990
Let me introduce University of California
professor Jeffrey Brantingham.

00:10:52.990 --> 00:10:58.940
He is the brains behind the police's secret
new weapon, PredPol.

00:10:58.940 --> 00:11:04.450
Brantingham is an archaeologist, and in the
early 2000s he was in Tibet discovering how

00:11:04.450 --> 00:11:07.620
ancient civilisations found food.

00:11:07.620 --> 00:11:14.950
This led him to wonder why humans commit crimes
in the places they do, so he had an idea,

00:11:14.950 --> 00:11:20.339
instead of treating criminals like criminals
and crimes as crimes, he thought of criminals

00:11:20.339 --> 00:11:26.260
like scavengers and crimes as pieces of food
that they are trying to find.

00:11:26.260 --> 00:11:34.040
He used this logic to create a mathematical
algorithm, the details of how it works are

00:11:34.040 --> 00:11:41.579
a secret, but what it does is predict exactly
when and where a crime is likely to take place

00:11:41.579 --> 00:11:43.470
in realtime.

00:11:43.470 --> 00:11:48.450
PredPol uses very complex mathematics and
thousands of bits of data such as the general

00:11:48.450 --> 00:11:54.459
profile and history of a specific location
within an area and recent crimes and events,

00:11:54.459 --> 00:11:57.199
in order to make its predictions.

00:11:57.199 --> 00:12:01.360
Now you might automatically think that it
simply flags up areas where crimes usually

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:06.860
do take place such as gang-ridden areas and
red light districts and tells police officers

00:12:06.860 --> 00:12:14.120
to spend all their time patrolling that location,
but not at all.

00:12:14.120 --> 00:12:20.430
The time and location predictions PredPol
produces actually change many times every

00:12:20.430 --> 00:12:27.399
night and are very widespread, it could for
example flag up an area in a wealthy residential

00:12:27.399 --> 00:12:32.120
neighbourhood just a few square metres wide,
predicting a crime will happen there within

00:12:32.120 --> 00:12:38.620
the next hour, that's how incredibly intelligent
this system is.

00:12:38.620 --> 00:12:45.860
It was trailed in two locations with two police
forces, California and Kent in the UK.

00:12:45.860 --> 00:12:51.880
The police officers would simply check the
computer's latest prediction and patrol that

00:12:51.880 --> 00:12:57.490
area at the given time, the idea is that having
a police presence in an area where a crime

00:12:57.490 --> 00:13:02.709
could soon take place will most likely stop
it from happening.

00:13:02.709 --> 00:13:11.480
The results were incredible, in both locations
that PredPol was tested, crime fell significantly,

00:13:11.480 --> 00:13:18.440
by around 8%, and the algorithm was found
to be twice as accurate as human police officers

00:13:18.440 --> 00:13:21.750
at predicting when and where a crime will
happen next.

00:13:21.750 --> 00:13:27.529
Brantingham says the system works so well
because whilst crime seems like a random event,

00:13:27.529 --> 00:13:33.540
it is in fact not at all, there are many variables
and factors that go in to making a person

00:13:33.540 --> 00:13:39.110
want to commit a crime at a specific place
at a specific time.

00:13:39.110 --> 00:13:46.130
So here's a top tip, if you're a budding criminal,
decide where to commit your crime then suddenly

00:13:46.130 --> 00:13:52.200
change your mind, unless of course the computer
has already predicted that you would do that.

00:13:52.200 --> 00:14:07.149
Thanks for watching.

